  'czech-wolfdog':'Created in 1950s Czechoslovakia through a planned German Shepherd and Carpathian wolf breeding program, then developed as a working dog.',
  'bichon-frise':'A Mediterranean companion dog whose modern breed was developed in France and Belgium. It became popular in European courts and later as a performer.',
  'yorkshire-terrier':'Developed in 19th-century Yorkshire, England, by working-class weavers from terriers brought from Scotland and local terriers.',
  'tibetan-mastiff':'An ancient livestock and settlement guardian of the Tibetan Plateau. Written records of its early development are sparse, so precise ancestry is uncertain.',
  'maltese':'An ancient Mediterranean companion dog known in Greek and Roman sources. Despite its name, a precise place of origin has not been established.',
  'saint-bernard':'Developed by monks at the Great St Bernard Hospice in the Swiss Alps for mountain work and rescue. The dogs became famous for locating travelers in snow.',
  'dachshund':'Developed in Germany to enter burrows and hunt badgers. The name means “badger dog”; coat varieties and sizes were later established for different work.',
